A||N||7||Seanh||Steering Rack Questions||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||08-09-2001||07:01 PM||||I just finally finished my car the Monday before last, and drove it till last Thrusday when a lady hit the front drivers side of my car and pushed its passenger side into the back of another car. My steering column was already screwed up from the last crash that I just fixed the car from. I couldn't find another one so I drove on it for a while. It really screwed it up this time, so I got another one comming. Anyway, the rack is a new (reman) one from Advance auto. I thought maybe the rack got screwed up again, so I pulled the column and turned the rack with pliers. It appears to move fine all the way around, except there is a little spot right in the middle where is appears to be missing a tooth I think. Is that normal, or is it really missing a tooth? I would think I could normally take it back to advance, but I don't know where the reciept is, so there went that idea. Is there anyway I can fix it?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||08-09-2001||07:55 PM||||My 88 has a dead spot in the reman rack I put in a couple of years ago.  I talked with Ed Parks at Williamsburg and he suspects that the rack may have a broken tooth.  When the racks get rebuilt, new busings get put in.  Not much else is checked.  An 88 has a unavailable replacement rack.  But Ed would send me one for a price.  Fiero store is out, FOCOA needs 88 racks for rebuilding, none available.<P>I only know of replacement of racks, since I'm not into rebuilding them myself.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-09-2001||08:04 PM||||Did advance ask for your name and phone number? They usually do on large stuff. If so they have a record in the computer of your purchase for warrantee.||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||08-09-2001||08:04 PM||||6 months ago, Autozone had remanufactured racks for 84-87's, under $150, either ratio.  Lot's cheaper than any other parts place, especially for the quick ratio.||||reg||1
Z||000004||stomper282||08-09-2001||11:44 PM||||Is it possible to replace the inner tie rod ends on an 88 rack? Can any one tell me the major difference between an 84-87 rack appose to an 88 rack?<BR>              Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||08-09-2001||11:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stomper282:<BR><B>Is it possible to replace the inner tie rod ends on an 88 rack? Can any one tell me the major difference between an 84-87 rack appose to an 88 rack?<BR>              Mike</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>It is very possible (and cheap!) to replace the inner tie rods on an '88.  I just did this on my '88 GT.......new inner & outer tie rods, new rack bushing plus new balljoints, bushings, brakes, etc.<P>Rodney Dickman (on this forum) sells inner tie rods for $27......a STEAL, if you ask me.  I believe GM wants 3 times that amount (if they are still available).  <P><BR>There are several differences between the '88 rack and the '84-'87 racks, but the easiest thing to spot is the steering dampner (looks like a shock).  The '88 rack does not have this.....only the '84-'87 racks do.<P> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||stomper282||08-10-2001||12:01 AM||||Thanks for the info, I also heard a roumor about a power steering kit, any real info?? <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||08-10-2001||12:12 AM||||GM never adapted a power steering system.....it was supposed to have an electo-hydraulic rack in '88, but they killed the Fiero before it went into production.<P>There was a company selling retro-fit kits using a modified rack from another car (Merkur XR4Ti?), but I don't believe there are any current "kits" on the market.<P>My personal feeling is the '88 doesn't need power steering....if everything is in good working order it will steer pretty effortlessly.  Now the earlier Fieros could use some assistance!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
